function, so I can only fully describe the parts shown.
The prompt below covers what's visible and marks the unseen portion as unknown
rather than guessing at it.
Write a single self-contained Python 3 script (intended to run under Termux on
Android, so use a Termux-style shebang pointing to the Termux Python
interpreter) that prepares embedded font CSS for a document-rendering pipeline
that uses WeasyPrint.
Purpose: The script builds a CSS string containing @font-face rules that embed
Inter and JetBrains Mono font files as base64 data URIs, so that a WeasyPrint
HTML-to-PDF rendering step can use the fonts without depending on
system-installed typefaces.
Startup and imports: - Begin with the `from __future__ import annotations`
import for forward-compatible type hints. - Use only the standard library:
`base64` and `pathlib.Path`.
No third-party packages are required by this module itself.
Helper function, `get_font_b64_or_fallback(filename)`: - Accepts a filename
string, interpreted relative to the current working directory. - If the file
does not exist, print two warning lines to stdout: first a line starting with a
warning sign and the text that the named file was not found in the current
directory; second an indented line stating that WeasyPrint will fall back to
default system typography for this style.
Then return an empty string. - If the file exists, read its bytes, base64-encode
them, decode to a UTF-8 string, and return that string. - Do not raise an
exception on a missing font; missing fonts are a soft failure.
Main CSS builder, `build_precise_css()`: - Print a progress message such as
"Parsing typography file tree..." to stdout. - Load these five font files, in
this order, each through the helper above: 1.
Inter-Regular.ttf 2.
Inter-Bold.ttf 3.
Inter-Italic.ttf 4.
Inter-BoldItalic.ttf 5.
JetBrainsMono-Regular.ttf - Maintain a list of CSS `@font-face` blocks.
For each Inter variant whose base64 string is non-empty, append one block with:
- font-family set to 'Inter' - font-style normal for the regular and bold
variants, and italic for the italic and bold-italic variants - font-weight 400
for regular, 700 for bold, 400 for italic, and 700 for bold-italic - a src
descriptor using a data URI of the form
data:font/truetype;charset=utf-8;base64,<payload> with format('truetype') - Use
Python f-strings with doubled braces for the CSS curly braces, and format each
block with consistent four-space indentation inside the braces. - Skip any block
whose base64 payload is empty, so a missing font simply produces no rule. - The
JetBrains Mono variant is handled the same way, with a font-family of 'JetBrains
Mono' (or an equivalent monospace family name) and weight 400 (the remainder of
this function was not provided in the source and should be completed in the same
pattern: build the mono block, join all blocks with newlines, and append any
further base-level CSS rules for body and code typography, then return the
complete CSS string).
Edge cases and behavior to preserve: - Missing files produce warnings, not
crashes; the build continues with whatever fonts are present. - If no fonts are
found at all, the function must still return a valid (possibly empty or minimal)
CSS string. - Output is printed to stdout only; there is no file writing in the
visible portion. - Reading uses binary mode so font bytes are preserved exactly.
Usage: - The script is intended to be run from the directory containing the font
files, since paths are relative to the current working directory. - Expose
`build_precise_css()` so other modules can import and call it to obtain the CSS
string for WeasyPrint's stylesheet input. - Include a `if __name__ ==
"__main__":` guard that calls the builder and, if desired, prints the resulting
CSS length or a summary.
Deliverable: one complete, runnable Python file implementing exactly the
behavior above, with clear function docstrings or brief comments, and no
external dependencies. ---
